  "query_preview": "..\n(This evening through Saturday)\nIssued at 408 PM CDT SUN OCT 25 2026\n\nThis afternoon a large high pressure ridge extends across Quebec\nand New England, south into the Deep South, and westward into\nTexas. In the upper levels, northerly flow continues across\nCentral Alabama with an upper ridge centered over the Mid and\nLower Mississippi River Valleys with one low to the west over\nUtah and Arizona and a second low just off the Atlantic Coast.\nDry conditions will persist through Friday with warm days and\ncool nights. A surface low is present across the far northern\nportions of Eastern Canada today with an associated front\nextending out to the southwest of it across the Great Lakes and\ninto Kansas. Upper ridging will traverse out of Central Conus to\nthe Southeast US Monday into Tuesday. We will remain initially in\nan overall rain free pattern with little available moisture for\nrain, but we will likely see some fair weather clouds for Monday\nthrough Wednesday with some minor perturbations in the upper\nflow. However, the lower levels will remain relatively dry for\nthe first half of the week with no major frontal system set to\nmove through the area through the forecast. Temperatures will\nremain fairly similar through Wednesday with lows in the 40s to\nthe lower 50s and highs generally in the 70s across Central\nAlabama. Toward the end of the week, our low level flow begins to\nturn more east-southeast. This will allow for a little moderating\nof our airmass with more 50s and maybe even a few 60s and some\nlower 80s mingling in with the respective lows and highs by\nThursday into next weekend. Low rain chances occur by next\nweekend as overall moisture increases.\n\n08",
